  "fixed_mapping_failure_pattern_note": "A fake/fixed-mapping agent would hard-code a rule like 'if the NEW_DEVICE tag is present, always call get_device_history then get_linked_entities' -- it would branch 10/10 times for a reason that has nothing to do with what get_device_history actually returned, and it would branch identically even if the device_history fixture were changed to show no cross-account signal at all. This implementation never encodes get_linked_entities as a required or expected follow-up anywhere in the system prompt or in code: shared/evidence/floor.py's minimum-evidence floor for NEW_DEVICE requires only get_device_history, and the agent's system prompt (shared/evidence/agent.py, _system_instruction) only gives the general instruction to follow up on 'a relationship...that looks materially relevant', which names no specific tool and would apply identically to any anomaly tag. Whether Gemini calls get_linked_entities is decided at inference time, by reading the actual device_risk_signals content (SEEN_ON_DIFFERENT_ACCOUNT) returned by the real get_device_history tool call -- if that signal were absent from the fixture, there would be nothing in the conversation prompting the follow-up. That is what makes this genuinely agentic rather than a fixed mapping, and it is exactly why this proof requires repeated real runs (this script) rather than resting on a single successful run.",
